PART 2: UNDERSTANDING THE map() FUNCTION

================================================================================

What is map()?

map() is a JavaScript array method.

Characteristic   | Description
-----------------|--------------------------------------------
Array Method     | Works on arrays
Iteration        | Processes every array element
Transformation   | Converts each item into another value
Return Value     | Returns a new array
Original Array   | Does not modify the original array itself
React Usage      | Commonly transforms data into JSX elements


================================================================================

map() SYNTAX

================================================================================

array.map((item, index, array) => {
  // Process item
  return transformedItem;
});

Parameters
Parameter | Description
----------|------------------------------------------
item      | Current array element
index     | Current position: 0, 1, 2, ...
array     | Original array being processed


In React, the most common forms are:

array.map(item => ...)


or:

array.map((item, index) => ...)


================================================================================

BASIC map() EXAMPLE - NON-REACT

================================================================================

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

const doubled =
  numbers.map(num => num * 2);

console.log(doubled);

Output
[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

Flow
Original Array
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
       ↓
map(num => num * 2)
       ↓
Process Every Number
       ↓
New Array
[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

Original Array | [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
New Array      | [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]


map() returns a new array rather than changing the original array itself.

================================================================================

map() WITH OBJECTS

================================================================================

const books = [
  {
    id: 1,
    title: 'Book 1'
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    title: 'Book 2'
  },
  {
    id: 3,
    title: 'Book 3'
  }
];

const titles =
  books.map(book => book.title);

console.log(titles);

Output
['Book 1', 'Book 2', 'Book 3']

Explanation
Current Object               | Returned Value
-----------------------------|---------------
{id: 1, title: 'Book 1'}     | Book 1
{id: 2, title: 'Book 2'}     | Book 2
{id: 3, title: 'Book 3'}     | Book 3

Flow
BOOK OBJECTS
     ↓
map(book => book.title)
     ↓
EXTRACT title
     ↓
ARRAY OF TITLES


