PART 3: USING map() IN REACT

================================================================================

Basic map() in JSX
export default function BookList() {

  const books = [
    {
      id: 1,
      title: 'The Great Gatsby'
    },
    {
      id: 2,
      title: '1984'
    },
    {
      id: 3,
      title: 'To Kill a Mockingbird'
    }
  ];

  return (
    <div>

      <h1>Books</h1>

      <ul>

        {books.map(book => (

          <li key={book.id}>
            {book.title}
          </li>

        ))}

      </ul>

    </div>
  );
}

Code Explanation
Code              | Purpose
------------------|---------------------------------------------
books             | Array containing book objects
books.map(...)    | Processes every book
book              | Current book object
<li>              | JSX created for each book
{book.title}      | Displays current book title
key={book.id}     | Gives current item a stable identity

Output
Books

- The Great Gatsby
- 1984
- To Kill a Mockingbird

React List Flow
books[]
   ↓
map()
   ↓
Book 1 → <li>The Great Gatsby</li>
Book 2 → <li>1984</li>
Book 3 → <li>To Kill a Mockingbird</li>
   ↓
ARRAY OF REACT ELEMENTS
   ↓
RENDERED LIST


