PART 6: THE key PROP

================================================================================

What is key?

A key provides identity to each sibling element in a rendered list.

React uses keys during reconciliation.

Purpose          | Explanation
-----------------|----------------------------------------------
Identity         | Distinguishes sibling items
Reconciliation   | Helps match previous and new list items
Insert/Delete    | Helps identify added or removed items
Reordering       | Helps preserve correct item identity
Component State  | Stable identity helps preserve state correctly

Without key
{books.map(book => (

  <li>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}


React normally gives a warning about unique keys.

With key
{books.map(book => (

  <li key={book.id}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}


================================================================================

GOOD VS BAD KEYS

================================================================================

Best: Stable ID from Data
{books.map(book => (

  <li key={book.id}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}

Sometimes Acceptable: Index for a Static List
{books.map((book, index) => (

  <li key={index}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}


Use carefully when the items will not be reordered, inserted, or deleted.

Avoid: No Key
{books.map(book => (

  <li>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}

Avoid: Random Key
{books.map(book => (

  <li key={Math.random()}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}


Math.random() creates a different key across renders, preventing reliable item matching.

Quick Comparison
Key           | Recommendation | Reason
--------------|----------------|----------------------------------
book.id       | Preferred      | Stable identity from data
index         | Use carefully  | Represents position
No key        | Avoid          | No explicit stable identity
Math.random() | Avoid          | Key changes between renders


================================================================================

WHEN INDEX AS KEY CAN CAUSE PROBLEMS

================================================================================

Example:

const [items, setItems] = useState([
  'Apple',
  'Banana',
  'Orange'
]);

{items.map((item, index) => (

  <li key={index}>
    {item}
  </li>

))}

Initial Data
Key/Index | Item
----------|--------
0         | Apple
1         | Banana
2         | Orange


Remove Banana:

Key/Index | Item
----------|--------
0         | Apple
1         | Orange


Now Orange receives key 1, which previously identified Banana.

For simple text this may not be noticeable. For stateful components, form controls, animations, or reordered lists, position-based keys can cause React to associate existing component state with the wrong data item.

