PART 9: map() VS OTHER ARRAY METHODS

================================================================================

1. map() - Transform Every Item
const numbers = [
  1,
  2,
  3
];

const doubled =
  numbers.map(n => n * 2);


Result:

[2, 4, 6]


Flow:

EVERY INPUT ITEM
      ↓
TRANSFORM
      ↓
NEW OUTPUT ITEM
      ↓
NEW ARRAY


================================================================================

2. filter() - Keep Matching Items

================================================================================

const books = [
  {
    title: 'Book 1',
    price: 12.99
  },
  {
    title: 'Book 2',
    price: 24.99
  },
  {
    title: 'Book 3',
    price: 11.99
  }
];

const affordable =
  books.filter(
    book => book.price < 15
  );

Result
Book 1
Book 3


================================================================================

3. find() - Find the First Matching Item

================================================================================

const books = [
  {
    id: 1,
    title: 'Book 1'
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    title: 'Book 2'
  },
  {
    id: 3,
    title: 'Book 3'
  }
];

const book =
  books.find(
    b => b.id === 2
  );

Result
{
  id: 2,
  title: 'Book 2'
}


================================================================================

4. forEach() - Perform an Operation for Every Item

================================================================================

const books = [
  {
    title: 'Book 1'
  },
  {
    title: 'Book 2'
  }
];

books.forEach(book => {

  console.log(
    book.title
  );

});


forEach() itself returns undefined.

Therefore, map() is more directly useful when you need a new array of JSX elements.

